  "query_preview": "..(This Afternoon Through Saturday)\nIssued at 1148 AM MST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nCurrent water vapor satellite shows a closed upper low spinning\nover northern Minnesota, moving east to the northern Great Lakes\nregion. Visible imagery shows a cold front draped from out in front\nof the upper low draped southwest down through the southern Great\nPlains. Temperatures across the area are in the 20s, reaching \njust above freezing in central South Dakota and along the Nebraska\nborder. \n\nHigh pressure will continue to build in across the northern Great\nPlains. On Monday, as the area of high pressure moves eastward,\npressure falls up against the Rockies will form a strong surface \ngradient over the area, supporting gusty conditions. Gusts up to\n30mph are expected Monday afternoon, and are most likely across\nthe South Dakota plains. Temperatures will not rebound much, with\nhighs in the 30s for the vast majority of the area. An upper level\nwave will spin up a mini Alberta system that will move through the\narea on Wednesday. It will be a dry and weak system, and nothing\nmore than a minor wind shift is expected from it. \n\nA warming trend is expected through the week. Highs for Mon/Tue \nwill be in the 40s, reaching up into the 50s by the weekend. This \nwill be 5-10 degrees above seasonal averages. Our next chance of\nprecipitation will come this weekend, although as it sits right\nnow the likelihood of good precip is low.",
